    overview:
      "Welcome to intellectual property law. This field protects four genuinely different kinds of intangible asset, each with its own eligibility test, its own scope of protection, and its own built-in limits, and the single most common mistake a newcomer makes is treating all four as interchangeable. Over the next forty minutes you will work through ten ideas in a deliberate order. We start with patents: what makes an invention eligible at all, how a claim's scope gets compared against an accused product, and who actually owns an invention once it exists, including the licensing rules that apply when a patent becomes essential to an industry standard. We then turn to trademarks: how a mark earns protection in the first place, what counts as infringement, and the two ways an owner can actually lose rights it once had. We turn next to copyright: what qualifies for protection and what never does, who owns a work once it is created and what a license over it actually permits, and the exceptions and personal rights that survive even a full transfer of the economic rights. We close with trade secrets, the one major form of intellectual property that requires no registration at all and lasts only as long as the information actually stays secret. Keep one question running underneath everything you hear: which of these four regimes actually fits the asset in front of you, since reaching for the wrong one is the single most common error in this entire field.",
