        explanation:
          "A patent protects an invention, not an idea, and three separate requirements have to be met before anything is patentable at all. First, the invention has to be eligible subject matter under 35 U.S.C. § 101: a process, machine, manufacture, or composition of matter, but not an abstract idea, a law of nature, or a natural phenomenon standing alone [1]. The Supreme Court's two-step framework from Alice Corp. v. CLS Bank and Mayo v. Prometheus asks first whether a claim is directed to one of these excluded categories, and if so, whether the claim nonetheless adds an 'inventive concept' sufficient to transform the abstract idea into a patent-eligible application, such as a specific technical improvement rather than merely applying the idea on a generic computer [2]. Second, the invention has to be novel under § 102: not identical to any single prior-art reference, with a one-year grace period for the inventor's own pre-filing disclosures. Third, it has to be non-obvious under § 103: not merely a predictable combination of what a person of ordinary skill in the art would already have found obvious, the standard clarified in KSR v. Teleflex, which rejected a rigid test for combining references in favor of a flexible, common-sense inquiry [1].",
