    connections:
      "Ten ideas, four regimes, one underlying discipline: matching the right legal tool to the right kind of intangible asset. Patents reward genuine technical invention disclosed to the public in exchange for a time-limited monopoly, which is why eligibility, novelty, and non-obviousness all have to be cleared before ownership and licensing questions even matter, and why a standard-essential patent's FRAND commitment exists to stop that monopoly from being leveraged into holdup. Trademarks protect not invention but a signal, the link between a mark and its source, which is why the distinctiveness spectrum determines what can be protected at all, confusion and dilution define what counts as infringing that signal, and genericide is the law's reminder that a signal the public no longer actually uses as a signal stops being protectable. Copyright protects creative expression, never the idea or system underneath it, which is why the idea-expression dichotomy has to be applied honestly before any infringement analysis even starts, why ownership defaults can surprise a commissioning party with no signed agreement, and why fair use and the narrow scope of US moral rights both reflect the same underlying choice to keep the economic right to control a work narrower and more alienable than most other legal systems allow. Trade secrets, finally, protect secrecy itself rather than any registered right, trading the certainty and disclosure of a patent for a protection that can last forever, provided, and only provided, the holder actually keeps the information secret through genuinely reasonable measures. Notice the throughline: every one of these regimes trades some form of certainty for some form of freedom, a registered, disclosed, time-limited patent right against open competition once it expires; a durable trademark against the constant risk of losing it through inattention; an easily obtained copyright against a shallow scope of protection for everything but the specific expression; and an indefinite trade secret against total loss the moment secrecy actually fails.",
