    connections:
      "Ten ideas, each shaped by the same two forces running beneath German intellectual property law: a civil-law tradition built on enumerated, codified rules rather than open-ended balancing tests, and an increasingly thick layer of EU harmonization sitting on top of it. Patent requirements, claim scope, and exhaustion show the EPC and EU single-market overlay directly, while the Arbeitnehmererfindungsgesetz and Germany's dominance in FRAND litigation show how a genuinely national statute and a genuinely European doctrinal framework can operate side by side within the same dispute. Trademark distinctiveness, confusion, and the limits on even a valid mark all track EU harmonization closely, often down to the identical multi-factor tests EU courts have developed, while still running through the national MarkenG's specific numbered provisions. Urheberrecht is where the civil-law character comes through most sharply and most consequentially: a closed list of enumerated Schranken rather than a flexible fair-use balancing test, an Urheberrecht that can never actually be sold, and a robust, persistent Urheberpersönlichkeitsrecht that survives any exploitation agreement, all of it a world apart from how a common-law system like the one you may be more used to treats the same underlying creative output. Trade secrets close the lecture by showing the EU-harmonization pattern one final time, a 2019 statute, directly implementing an EU directive, that replaced an older, more purely national body of case law with a more demanding, more predictable objective standard. Miss either the codified, enumerated structure or the EU overlay running beneath any one of these ten ideas, and you will reach for the wrong tool, or the wrong jurisdiction's doctrine, at exactly the moment it matters.",
