          "Ordinary trademark infringement under Articles L713-2 and L713-3 CPI requires a likelihood of confusion (risque de confusion), weighed through the same EU-wide interdependence principle applied throughout the harmonized European system: similarity of signs, similarity of goods or services, and the distinctiveness of the earlier mark are assessed together rather than as independent pass/fail thresholds. Separately, Article L713-5 CPI protects a mark with a reputation (marque renommée) against use that takes unfair advantage of its distinctive character or reputation, or causes it prejudice, even for dissimilar goods, requiring no likelihood of confusion at all, the French and EU functional equivalent of US dilution, but again demanding only reputation among a significant part of the relevant public, a lower bar than the US nationwide-fame standard.",
