      "Ten ideas, all shaped by the same author-centered philosophy running through French intellectual property law, even where EU harmonization has smoothed over many of the surface-level differences with its neighbors. Patent conditions, infringement, and exhaustion track the EPC and EU single market closely, the same as in Germany, but France's own three-category scheme for employee inventions, mission, hors mission attribuable, and hors mission non attribuable, shows a distinctly French granularity that a binary scope-of-employment rule does not capture. Trademark distinctiveness, confusion, and renown-based protection run through EU-harmonized tests nearly identical to Germany's, while the limits on even a valid mark, dégénérescence and the Article L713-6 limitations, show the same policing-conduct logic appearing across every harmonized European system. Droit d'auteur is where French law's distinctive character shows most sharply: a closed list of enumerated exceptions rather than open-ended fair use, a formalistic, restrictively interpreted rule for assigning even the patrimonial rights that can be transferred, and, above all, a droit moral the Code itself declares perpetual, inalienable, and imprescriptible, the strongest, most durable moral-rights tradition among the systems covered in this series. Le secret des affaires closes the lecture by showing the same EU-harmonization pattern Germany's Geschäftsgeheimnisgesetz shows, a recent statute transposing the identical EU directive, though housed, tellingly, in French commercial law rather than alongside the CPI's author-centered intellectual property regime. Carry the throughline forward: wherever French law lets a right be bought, sold, or licensed, ask what it still refuses to let go of, and you will usually find the author, or her legitimate interest in the work, standing behind the answer.",
