    connections:
      "Ten ideas, each shaped by the same pattern that runs through everything distinctly Swedish in this lecture: several separate, purpose-built statutes rather than one code, a deep reliance on förarbeten and collective bargaining to fill in what the statutory text leaves general, and, sitting on top of all of it, the same EU harmonization layer reshaping Germany, France, and Spain's own systems in parallel. Patent requirements, infringement, and exhaustion track the EPC and EU single market exactly as they do everywhere else in this series, but Sweden's ratification of the Unitary Patent Court Agreement is a genuinely significant, concrete structural fact, not a minor footnote, that materially changes the litigation risk profile for any Swedish business holding or facing a European patent today. The 1949 employee-inventions act, considerably older and more principles-based than its German counterpart, leans unusually heavily on sector collective agreements to do work the statute's own text leaves general, a distinctly Swedish labor-market feature. Varumärke law runs through the same EU-harmonized tests as Germany, France, and Spain, with inarbetning as Sweden's own additional, genuinely Nordic route to unregistered rights. Upphovsrätt is where the most genuinely distinctive Swedish features concentrate: a more flexible, Nordic-pragmatic default for transferring economic rights generally, a specific, more US-like employer default carved out just for computer programs under § 40a, the avtalslicens extended collective licensing system with no real equivalent among its civil-law neighbors, and a moral right that, unlike France's and Spain's, can actually be waived for a sufficiently limited use. Företagshemligheter closes the lecture exactly where the German, French, and Spanish trade-secret statutes do, on the same EU-harmonized definition. Carry forward the lesson this entire comparative series has been building toward: shared EU harmonization does not mean uniform national law, and the real expertise lies in knowing exactly where, as with Sweden's avtalslicens or its UPC ratification, a system keeps something genuinely its own.",
